  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 AM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nPrecipitation/Storm Trends...Isolated showers prevailed across \nportions of central and east-central WI early this morning where an \n850mb jet was increasing within an area of WAA, while a narrow line \nof elevated thunderstorms developed over the WI/Upper MI border and \nexited the region to the northeast where an area of steeper mid-\nlevel lapse rates, 850mb f-gen, and another area of 850mb WAA \nprevailed. As the 850mb jet continues to increase within the area of \nWAA over central and east-central WI this morning, anticipate the \nshower activity increasing into northeast WI before exiting east by \n9 AM. Despite up to 1000 J/kg of MUCAPE and effective shear around \n30 kts, expecting only a few elevated, non-severe storms within the \nshower activity. If anything, the showers will produce brief heavy \ndownpours with PWATs near 2.00 inches.\n\nOnce the morning shower activity ends, much of today will be dry as \na weak capping inversion will be in place. However, some CAMs/short-\nrange models indicating a brief period when the cap may break during \nthe afternoon/evening producing isolated showers/storms as a weak \nlow-pressure system and associated weak cold front move over the \nregion. If this cap can break, convection will develop in a rather \nunstable and moist atmosphere with MUCAPE of 2000-3000 J/kg, deep-\nlayer shear of 35-45 kts, and dew points in the upper 60s to low \n70s. If any storms can develop, there will be a marginal threat for \nstrong/severe storms capable of producing hail around 1 inch and \ndamaging winds, in addition to torrential downpours. SPC has a \nMarginal risk for severe storms over much of the forecast area, \nexcept north-central WI. A few elevated storms may linger into the \nevening, but coverage looks limited.\n\nThe aforementioned weak cold front/boundary will sag south into \nTuesday and hover just to the south of the forecast area for \nTuesday, leaving dry conditions in place for most of the day. \nChances for showers and storms then return Tuesday evening/night as \nthe frontal boundary lifts north aided by mid-level forcing from an \napproaching mid-level shortwave. Shower/storm chances will continue \ninto Wednesday as the shortwave moves over the region. A couple \nshortwaves then look to move over the region through the remainder \nof the work week and into the start of the weekend, bringing \nintermittent chances for showers and storms at times.\n\nTemperatures/Humidity...Summer continues! A rather hot and humid day \nis expected with dew points in the upper 60s to low 70s as high \ntemperatures climb into the mid 80s to low 90s. This will result in \nafternoon heat indices in the low to mid 90s. Some spots may even \nsee heat indices in the upper 90s if temps or dewpoints slightly \nover perform. Despite the cold front moving over the region this \nevening, temperatures on Tuesday will be only a few degrees lower \nthan today. It will also still be humid on Tuesday, as dew points \nremain in the 60s to low 70s, but locations in north-central WI may \nsee a little relief with dew points forecast in the low 60s.",
